a warm welcome back to CBS mornings we  all know the holiday travel season is  here but there is one hidden danger you  may not be thinking about as you head  out of town and that's carbon monoxide  you'll want to listen up to this three  Americans died recently in Mexico City  and there are now renewed calls for  tighter regulations to keep Travelers  safe our Tanya Rivero has this story  my son called me and he said Candace is  no longer with us and I just  I lost it Candace Florence Jordan  Marshall and Cortez Hall had traveled to  Mexico City for Day of the Dead  festivities but the three friends were  found unresponsive in their Airbnb  rental authorities say from an apparent  carbon monoxide poisoning it was a  heartbreaking situation one death under  abuse one death too many Airbnb CEO  Brian chesky addressed the tragedy last  week on CBS mornings we will send free  coo detectors to anyone on Airbnb that  needs one we've already sent 200 000 to  our community and I've told our team  we're going to get even more aggressive  to make sure everyone complies carbon  monoxide is not often top of mind for  travelers no matter where they're going  or where they're staying in May  officials in the Bahamas confirmed three  American tourists died of carbon  monoxide poisoning at a resort there  were some signs of individuals  presenting to the clinic with nausea  vomiting and some symptoms carbon  monoxide is an odorless colorless toxic  gas emitted by combusting Fuel the  symptoms of Co poisoning are flu-like  headache dizziness nausea only 14 states  have CO detector regulations for hotels  if people knew the truth about not just  how many states have requirements but  how convoluted the requirements are I  think they would be very surprised at  the lack of protection we have in 2013  Chris hauschult lost both of her parents  to carbon monoxide poisoning in a North  Carolina hotel seven weeks later an  11-year-old boy died in the same room  it's just incredible and the fact that a  third person a child had to die in that  same room before anyone took action that  should be happening at all in response  hauschult founded the Jenkins Foundation  a non-profit that tracks carbon monoxide  incidents at U.S hotels we had no idea  kind of this scope of it and that it was  a danger according to the foundation's  data just over a thousand people have  been injured by carbon monoxide leaks in  hotels over the past two decades and  more than 30 have died Health experts  say this is likely a severe under count  since so many Hotel incidents go  unreported the thing that makes me most  upset is that it's preventable Dr Andrew  Moffett is a hyperbaric specialist at  Intermountain Healthcare in Salt Lake  City Utah if you had a small piece of  equipment that was installed correctly  by people who were careful then these  people wouldn't die or get extremely  sick  in a statement to CBS News the American  Hotel and Lodging Association said in  part all hotels are required to follow  applicable state and local fire  Mechanical Code and Co detection  requirements ahla encourages members to  include CO detectors in hotel rooms and  spaces with fuel fired appliances  experts say to protect yourself when you  travel bring your own battery operated  CO detector with you you can easily buy  one online guys I bought this one for  myself online after doing this story I  carry it with me everywhere this one you  can plug in and it also has batteries in  case power goes out how much is that  very good it was this one was about 30  bucks so they range in cost 30 bucks is  the low end a little cheaper but yeah it  could save your life good advice thank  you Tanya appreciate it
 

